  "unit": "u10-culture",
  "title": "With close friends",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "**তুই** is the language of real closeness: best friends, siblings and cousins close in age, and often parents to young children. Among young Bangladeshi friends you'll hear **দোস্ত** ('buddy, mate') all the time. Used with a stranger or someone older, তুই is rude, so only use it with someone your own age (or younger) who already uses it with you. An elder may call you তুই, but you still answer with **আপনি** or **তুমি**.\n\n'Let's…' = **চল / চলো / চলেন** + the আমি-form of the verb. Pick the one that matches the person:\n\n- **চল, মেলায় যাই** — 'Let's go to the fair' (তুই)\n- **চলো, যাই** — 'Let's go' (তুমি)\n- **চলেন, চা খাই** — 'Shall we have tea?' (আপনি)\n\nThe তুই future ends in *-bi*: **আসবি?** 'Will you come?', **কী খাবি?** 'What'll you have?' Compare **আসবে** (তুমি) and **আসবেন** (আপনি) from the last lesson.\n\n**হবে** 'will be / will happen' is the future of 'be': **আজ নাচ আর গান হবে** 'There'll be dancing and singing today'.\n\n**Kolkata:** polite **চলুন** (*cholun*) instead of চলেন; **দোস্ত** is rare there.",
